faq:
  - q: "What is ByteDance's hiring process in 2026?"
    a: "ByteDance (TikTok's parent company) has a structured technical hiring process for US and international roles. Typical flow: (1) Recruiter screen — 20–30 minutes on background, compensation, and role alignment. (2) Online coding assessment — typically 2 algorithm problems in 60–90 minutes (LeetCode medium to hard). (3) Technical phone screen — 1 coding round with an engineer (45–60 minutes). (4) Onsite or virtual onsite — 4–6 rounds including 2–3 algorithm coding rounds, 1 system design round, 1 behavioral/leadership round, and for senior+ roles, an additional domain-specific or architecture round. Total process runs 4–8 weeks. ByteDance/TikTok hiring was significantly impacted by regulatory uncertainty in 2024–2025, so confirm current hiring status in your target team."
  - q: "What does ByteDance look for in engineers?"
    a: "ByteDance has one of the highest algorithm interview bars of any tech company. For engineering roles: (1) Algorithm mastery — the coding bar exceeds Google in some teams; LeetCode hard problems are common and expected to be solved cleanly under time pressure. (2) Systems at scale — ByteDance operates some of the world's highest-traffic systems; experience with distributed systems, content delivery, feed ranking, or recommendation systems is highly relevant. (3) Speed and output — ByteDance's engineering culture moves faster than US Big Tech; candidates who demonstrate rapid iteration and high output are preferred. (4) Low-level optimization — video processing, mobile performance, and real-time streaming are core to TikTok; experience with performance-critical code is a differentiator."
  - q: "How hard are ByteDance algorithm interviews?"
    a: "ByteDance algorithm interviews are among the hardest in the industry. The standard is LeetCode medium to hard — but the expectation is not just to reach a solution, but to produce clean, optimized code with correct time and space complexity analysis, discussed clearly under pressure. Common topics: dynamic programming, graph algorithms (BFS/DFS, shortest path, topological sort), tree manipulation, segment trees, binary search variants, and string algorithms. Two to three algorithm rounds in the onsite are standard, with progressively harder problems. Candidates without 200+ LeetCode problems practiced at the medium-hard level routinely fail the algorithm screens regardless of their practical engineering experience."
  - q: "What are ByteDance product manager interviews like?"
    a: "ByteDance PM interviews focus on product thinking with a data and algorithm lens — unusual compared to US Big Tech PM interviews. Expect: product design questions for consumer social and short-video products (how would you improve TikTok's recommendation algorithm? how would you design a creator monetization feature?), metrics and A/B testing questions (how do you measure a feed ranking change?), and cross-functional leadership scenarios. ByteDance PMs are expected to have stronger data and technical fluency than typical US product roles — candidates who can discuss algorithm tradeoffs, content moderation at scale, and creator ecosystem dynamics outperform those with purely business-side experience."
  - q: "What does ByteDance pay in 2026?"
    a: "ByteDance compensation is competitive with top-tier FAANG. Software Engineer (equivalent to L4-L5): $180–280K TC including base, cash bonus, and RSUs. Senior Software Engineer (L6-equivalent): $280–400K TC. Staff Engineer: $380–550K TC. Product Manager: $200–350K TC depending on seniority. ByteDance uses a combination of base salary, performance bonus, and Restricted Stock Units (RSUs). The RSUs are in ByteDance stock (not publicly traded in the US), which introduces liquidity risk. For TikTok US entity roles, some compensation structures use dollar-denominated RSUs with structured liquidity provisions."
  - q: "What are the regulatory risks of working at ByteDance or TikTok in 2026?"
    a: "Working at ByteDance or TikTok in the United States carries regulatory uncertainty that does not exist at other employers. Key considerations: potential forced divestiture or operational restrictions under US national security law, data access and security clearance implications for candidates who hold or seek government clearances (employment at ByteDance may be incompatible), reputational considerations for candidates targeting government or defense careers after TikTok, and business continuity risk if regulatory action accelerates. For engineers who are purely in the private sector and not seeking clearances, the financial upside and engineering scale opportunity are real — but the uncertainty is genuine and worth factoring into a long-term career decision."
